                <pb facs="0618" n="506" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg506"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/894: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reinsch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                            notAfter="1919-06-14T06:30:00-04:00"
                            notBefore="1919-06-12T16:00:00+04:53"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >undated</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received June 14, 1919, 6:30
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Following from Harris, Tomsk.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“26 [<hi rendition="#italic">260?</hi>]. June 12, 4 p.m. Following from
                        American Vice Consul, Chita: <quote rend="blockquote">
                            <p>‘Trouble which threatened to be serious occurred at Verchneudinsk
                                June 9th and 10th between Americans and
                                    Semenoff forces, <pb facs="0619" n="507"
                                    xml:id="pg507"/> Colonel Morrow demanded
                                removal of Semenoff armoured train and called
                                out troops to enforce demand. Train was finally removed.
                                    Semenoff claims trouble was
                                misunderstanding, brought about purposely by Russian-Jewish
                                interpreters with American forces. He adds that he has now been
                                officially notified of Allied control of railroad, will see that
                                nothing of the kind occurs again and will do all in his power to
                                work in accord with Allied forces and railroad engineers. Mr.
                                    Stevens called upon
                                    Semenoff today and was given
                                assurance.’</p>
                        </quote></p>
                    <p>Minister Soukine informs me that this whole matter has been amicably settled.
                            [Harris]”</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Reinsch</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
